# sm-workflow:begin
# Managed by sm-workflow (wf sync). Repo-specific paths go BELOW the end marker —
# add your auth middleware and any other repo-owned rail files there.
# The dangerous-file list, per the decision owner's 2026-08-11 ruling (TASK-1844)
# as consolidated on 2026-08-13: the workflows directory whole, the gate's own
# rails, guard and actor-gate scripts plus the two files that arm them,
# migration scripts, and wrangler.toml. Everything else lands ungated — a
# routine change must not trip the codeowner gate.
#
# `/.github/workflows/` is a DIRECTORY gate, deliberately, and it replaced four
# release-verb globs (`*deploy*`, `*publish*`, `*push*`, `*release*`) plus
# `sm-*`. Per-file patterns can only describe files that already exist: a
# workflow added tomorrow under a name nobody predicted was born ungated and
# merged with no tap, then ran on the default branch holding GITHUB_TOKEN and
# the repo's secrets. No pattern can express "a file that did not exist
# yesterday", so the directory is the unit. Narrowing this back to per-file
# globs re-opens that hole; the coverage test pins it with a workflow filename
# matching none of the old globs.
#
# THE ENTRY BELOW IS CONDITIONAL (TASK-2854), and it is the only conditional
# line in this block. `wf sync` drops it when `.sm-workflow.conf` carries
# `workflow_paths_ruleset=on` — meaning BUG-2217's push ruleset restricts
# `.github/workflows/**/*` here. That ruleset holds the SAME property this
# directory gate was written for, including for files that do not exist yet,
# and it holds it structurally rather than by spending a human review on every
# workflow edit. Where the ruleset cannot exist the entry stays: GitHub push
# rules require a private or internal repository, so a public one keeps this as
# its only control (the fleet has one, sm-ui).
#
# The key is not self-asserted. `wf adopt --server` sets it only when it has
# applied the ruleset, and clears it on the public-repo skip; `wf doctor` reads
# the live ruleset and FAILS when the two disagree, so the flag cannot go stale
# and silently un-gate a repository whose ruleset was deleted. Removing that
# cross-check turns this line into an unverified claim.
/.github/CODEOWNERS                    @sprint-mode/reviewers
/.github/workflows/                    @sprint-mode/reviewers
/.githooks/                            @sprint-mode/reviewers
# The two files that ARM the gated scripts. `.claude/settings.json` is the only
# place `guard-git.sh` is registered, via hooks.PreToolUse — delete that stanza
# and the gated script stays byte-identical on disk and is never invoked. It
# also carries extraKnownMarketplaces and the pre-approved MCP verb allowlist.
# `.sm-workflow.conf` is the sole input to sm-workflow-drift.yml's verdict, and
# carries `landing_branches` (guard-git.sh substitutes it for `main`, so an edit
# stops blocking direct pushes), `hook_profile`, `sweep` and `agent_pilot`.
# Gating an enforcer while leaving its arming file open is a bypass, not a
# narrowing. The rest of /.claude/ stays routine.
/.claude/settings.json                 @sprint-mode/reviewers
/.sm-workflow.conf                     @sprint-mode/reviewers
/scripts/guard-git.sh                  @sprint-mode/reviewers
/scripts/hook-env.sh                   @sprint-mode/reviewers
/scripts/pr-sweep.py                   @sprint-mode/reviewers
/scripts/sm-deploy-result.py            @sprint-mode/reviewers
/scripts/sm-deploy-http.py              @sprint-mode/reviewers
/scripts/sm-deploy-incident.py          @sprint-mode/reviewers
/scripts/check-migration-numbers.sh    @sprint-mode/reviewers
/scripts/migration-dupe-allowlist.txt  @sprint-mode/reviewers
wrangler.toml                          @sprint-mode/reviewers
# sm-workflow:end
